  "response": "The web and API maintain separate `Plan` closed sets, and they have drifted:\n\n- API/Python: `free | pro | team | scale`\n- Web/TypeScript: `free | pro | enterprise`\n\nSo when the API returns or the user selects the newest `scale` tier, the web validator rejects it because `scale` is absent from its local `Plan` definition—even though the API considers it valid. `team` has the same problem, while `enterprise` has the inverse problem: the web accepts it but the API rejects it.\n\nTo prevent recurrence, make one contract authoritative and generate the other representation from it—ideally generate the TypeScript client/types from an OpenAPI or shared JSON Schema. Add a boundary contract test in CI that compares the accepted plan values (and routes/methods), plus run `crimes scan` as a drift gate. Merely updating both enums now fixes this incident but leaves the synchronization failure in place.\n\nThe route-drift findings are separate bugs: `PUT` versus backend `POST`, and `/api/workspaces` with no matching backend route. They could cause 405/404 failures, but they do not explain this validation error.",
